course: dictionary
lesson: 2295
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is meant. «Crimp» can mean to press something into small folds, to squeeze two things together, or to spoil something in an informal American phrase. The words around it show you which meaning fits.
explain: Ask yourself: what is being crimped? In cooking, you crimp the edge of a pie to seal it. In the example, «iron your face and crimp it» goes with ironing and a mangle, so the job is pressing little waves into something, not sealing anything. A quick check: swap in your meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Be careful with the noun too. «A crimp» is a small fold, wave or ridge, so a wave in someone's hair can be a crimp. Dictionaries list each meaning separately, so look for the one that matches the sentence.
fact: Crimp can be a verb or a noun: you can crimp something, or you can see a crimp in it.
fact: «Crimp» means to press something into small folds or ridges, such as pastry edges or hair.
fact: «Crimp» can also mean to squeeze two things together so they join, like a metal end on a wire.
fact: In informal American English, «put a crimp in» something means to spoil it or limit it.
fact: In the example, «crimp» means to press into little waves, and the words «mangle» and «iron» give you the clue.
exercise: «Crimp the edges of the pie with a fork.» What does crimp mean here? | To wash the pie dish clean | To make the pie bigger | *To press into small folds to seal the edge | To eat the crust slowly
exercise: «That put a crimp in our plans.» In US informal English, what does «put a crimp in» mean? | *To spoil or limit something | To fold the plans into a paper plane | To make the plans bigger
exercise: «The wave in her hair was a neat little crimp.» What is a crimp? | A loud noise | A kind of hat | *A small fold, wave or ridge | A large river
test: Electricians crimp metal connectors onto wires. What does crimp mean here? | To wash the wires clean | *To squeeze two things together to join them | To cut the wires into pieces | To paint the wires
test: The cook crimped the pie crust. Which meaning fits best? | Washed it in cold water | Ate it all quickly | *Pressed it into small folds
exam: In «iron your face and crimp it», what does crimp mean? | *To press it into little waves | To wash it clean | To paint it bright red | To fly away
